**Loop variable `id` shadows outer PTY session ID**

This PR renamed the iterator variable from `key` to `id` in all subscriber loop sites. Inside `create()`, the outer `const id = Identifier.create("pty", false)` (the PTY session ID, line 123) is now silently shadowed here. While the logic is correct today — every use of `id` inside the loop intentionally refers to the subscriber key — any future change that needs the outer session ID inside this callback (e.g. for logging or re-publishing) would silently read the wrong value. Consider renaming the loop variable back to a distinct name:

```suggestion
      for (const [subKey, ws] of session.subscribers.entries()) {
        if (ws.readyState !== 1) {
          session.subscribers.delete(subKey)
          continue
        }

        if (key(ws) !== subKey) {
          session.subscribers.delete(subKey)
          continue
        }

        try {
          ws.send(chunk)
        } catch {
          session.subscribers.delete(subKey)
        }
```

The same shadowing occurs in `remove()` at line 231, where the `id: string` function parameter is shadowed by the for-loop iterator.

**`closeIdleConnections` is redundant after `closeAllConnections`**

`closeAllConnections()` terminates every connection — idle and active alike. Calling `closeIdleConnections()` immediately after is a no-op. The `closeIdleConnections` call can be removed:

```suggestion
          if (close) {
            if ("closeAllConnections" in server && typeof server.closeAllConnections === "function") {
              server.closeAllConnections()
            }
          }
```

New sidebar items can open arbitrary URLs via `platform.openLink(href)` when the plugin-provided `href` is not a relative path. On web this ultimately calls `window.open(url, "_blank")`, which allows dangerous schemes like `javascript:`/`data:` and can lead to script execution or opener-based attacks. Consider whitelisting allowed schemes (e.g., http/https/mailto) and rejecting anything else before calling `openLink` (or always treating non-absolute paths as internal navigation).
```suggestion
                    if (href.startsWith("/")) {
                      navigate(href)
                      return
                    }

                    try {
                      const url = new URL(href)
                      const allowedProtocols = ["http:", "https:", "mailto:"]
                      if (allowedProtocols.includes(url.protocol)) {
                        platform.openLink(href)
                      }
                    } catch {
                      // Invalid URL; do not open
                    }
```

The route wraps `Plugin.trigger("ui.sidebar", ...)` in a single `.catch(...)`, so a single plugin throwing inside its sidebar hook causes the entire endpoint to return `{ items: [] }`, dropping contributions from all other plugins. If the goal is to aggregate items across plugins robustly, catch errors per-plugin hook invocation (log and continue) so healthy plugins still contribute items even when one fails.
```suggestion
      try {
        const output = await Plugin.trigger("ui.sidebar", {}, { items: [] as SidebarItem[] })
        const map = new Map<string, SidebarItem>()
        for (const item of output.items) map.set(item.id, item)
        const body = {
          items: [...map.values()].sort(sort),
        }
        return c.json(body)
      } catch (error) {
        log.error("plugin sidebar failed", { error })
        // Propagate the error so the framework can return an appropriate error response
        throw error
      }
```

**Unbounded infinite recursion when package has no bin entries**

`which` calls `add(pkg)`, then unconditionally recurses via `return which(pkg)`. If the package installs successfully but exposes zero bin entries (no `bin` field in `package.json`, or `binLinks` fails to create symlinks), `readdir` returns `[]` again and the function recurses forever, eventually crashing with a stack overflow.

A base-case guard is needed after the install attempt:

```suggestion
  export async function which(pkg: string) {
    const dir = path.join(directory(pkg), "node_modules", ".bin")
    const files = await readdir(dir).catch(() => [])
    if (!files.length) {
      await add(pkg)
      const filesAfterInstall = await readdir(dir).catch(() => [])
      if (!filesAfterInstall.length) throw new InstallFailedError({ pkg })
      return path.join(dir, filesAfterInstall[0])
    }
    return path.join(dir, files[0])
  }
```

